what Americans think of national  security they tend to think in terms of  military threats but national security  is also question of maintaining a  nation's way of life whether people have  the jobs and the goods that they're  accustomed to increasingly we have asked  that question in the context of  international trade many of the goods  that we buy come from abroad each day  about 6 million containers of the kind  that you can see behind me arrived in  the United States from other countries  carrying everything from television sets  to clothes to toys you name it that  globalized economy has turned out to be  both the challenge and an opportunity  for the United States the United States  has important comparative advantages we  have a vibrant tech sector and we have  the most fertile agricultural sector in  the world those two sectors true  international trade have generated  millions of American jobs but we also  have comparative disadvantages the wage  scale in the United States is much  higher than it is in countries like  China and India in Guatemala that means  that those Goods often can be produced  more cheaply abroad that's good for  consumers we pay less for the goods we  buy as a result but it also means that  millions of American jobs have been lost  to foreign firms the fact that global  trade involves winners and losers has  made it one of the most important policy  issues of our time
 

